Build the histogram of the child with fewer rows in CPU hist.

When a node splits, the CPU hist and approx tree methods build the histogram of one child and
compute the other by subtracting it from the parent's. The child to build was the one with the
smaller sum of Hessian, a stand-in for the one with fewer rows. With `multi:softprob`, whose
Hessian is |p - y| since dmlc/xgboost#12537 and so near zero for well-classified rows, the child
with less Hessian is often the larger one. Outside distributed training, the rows are already
partitioned into the children when their histograms are built, so this counts them and builds the
child with fewer rows. Distributed training keeps the Hessian rule. The histograms are the same up
to floating-point rounding of the subtraction, so a near-tie split could in principle change; the
models measured with and without it (winnowr's smoke tests, and upstream benchmarks at 1 to 30
threads) were byte-identical.

Source: nh13/xgboost commit b48c1186f (branch nh_hist-build-smaller-child, on dmlc master
31abd0e3b, code-identical to 293cad1a8; draft https://github.com/nh13/xgboost/pull/1). The src/
hunks are carried verbatim, including comment and copyright changes, so the patch stays the
upstream commit's and can be dropped when it merges. Its C++ tests of the rule are upstream and
are not run here (build.rs builds XGBoost's sources, not its tests).

Applied by build.rs to a copy of the vendored sources (paths are relative to the XGBoost root),
after patches 0001 to 0003.

